# remotify.run - remote command execution for this project

This project has a remote shell reachable via a relay at
**https://remotify.run** (or a self-hosted instance; check this project's
`.env` / `docker-compose.yml` if unsure). The remote is where the project
actually runs in staging/production; your local machine is not.

## Routing rule (IMPORTANT - read before using any shell tool)

Decide per-command where it should execute:

- **Goes to the REMOTE** (via `remote_exec` tool if available, otherwise via
  curl as described below): anything that asks about the project's remote
  environment - "the server", "production", "staging", "the deployed DB",
  "the live app logs", system state on the remote host, etc.

- **Goes to your LOCAL shell/Bash tool**: anything about this repo on the
  developer's machine - `git log`, `git status`, running local tests,
  reading files in the working tree, local build commands.

Do **not** fall back to the local tool for a remote question, and do not
push remote commands through the local tool. They run on different machines.

### Chain freely - don't ask per step

Iterative remote work is the common case: log analysis (grep, awk, sed,
head, tail, cut), narrowing a search, diagnosing a slow service with
ps/vmstat/netstat/iostat, poking around `/proc`, cross-checking outputs.
Run the sequence yourself, show the user the meaningful result or the
synthesis - do not copy each raw command back into chat and ask them to
run it. That manual copy-paste loop is exactly what this tool eliminates.
In supervised mode the operator already gets a `y/N` prompt per command
on the remote shell, so consent is covered; you do not need to also ask
the user again.

## If your host has MCP (and the remotify MCP server is wired in)

There is a `remote_exec(command)` tool. Call it for every remote-targeted
operation. Don't ask the user which tool to use; route silently.

Example:
> User: "check disk usage on the server"
> You: call `remote_exec("df -h")`, then show the output.

## If your host does not have MCP

Use your local Bash/shell tool to hit the HTTP API directly.

### Once per session

```bash
curl -s -X POST https://remotify.run/api/session
```

The JSON response contains:
- `key` - 32-char session key
- `remote_quickstart` - the single line to show the user so they can paste
  it on the remote shell. Show it to them verbatim and ask them to paste
  it on the remote. Supervised mode is default; they can append
  `?mode=auto` themselves for unattended operation.

Wait for the user to confirm they've pasted it before pushing commands.

### For every command

```bash
# 1. enqueue the command
curl -s --data-raw 'YOUR COMMAND HERE' https://remotify.run/cmd-KEY

# 2. poll for the result (listener may take time to approve)
while :; do
  CODE=$(curl -fs -o /tmp/.r -w '%{http_code}' https://remotify.run/result-KEY)
  [ "$CODE" = 200 ] && { cat /tmp/.r; break; }
  sleep 1
done
```

### Response-code hints

- **201** on push: queued. Pushing again while something is already queued
  replaces it (last-wins rotation); the previous push is archived.
- **200** on result fetch: output is in the body.
- **204** on result fetch: nothing queued yet, keep polling.
- **410**: the session expired (idle TTL, default 3h) or the key is
  unknown. Create a new session and re-instruct the user.

### What the operator sees on the remote

Every queued command is printed on the remote as `>>> <cmd>` before it runs,
and `<<< done (N bytes pushed back)` after the result is posted back. In
supervised mode a `y/N` prompt sits between those lines (any answer starting
with `y` / `Y` runs it; anything else is recorded as `[declined by operator]`).
The runner also exports a non-interactive env block (`DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive`,
`CI=true`, `TERM=dumb`, `PAGER=cat`, `SYSTEMD_PAGER=cat`, `GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT=0`)
so apt, pagers, and git don't hang waiting for TTY input.

## Security and trust

- Commands run as whatever user pasted the listener on the remote. Same
  trust model as `ssh user@host 'cmd'`. Do not recommend running the
  listener on a shell you would not SSH into.
- Output is raw bytes. Do not assume UTF-8 for binary or mixed-content
  commands.
- Do not flip the remote into auto mode (`?mode=auto`) without explicit
  user consent. Supervised is the safe default.

## When to avoid remote_exec / the remote entirely

- Interactive programs (`vim`, `less`, `sudo` prompts without NOPASSWD) -
  the relay is one-shot per command.
- Commands that stream indefinitely (`tail -f`, `top`) - they block the
  listener until the user interrupts it.

## Ending a session

```bash
curl -sX DELETE https://remotify.run/api/session/KEY
```

Or just let it expire. Everything queued under the session is discarded.
